{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli lived from 1140 to 1187 and was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III's reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim states in the region and internal strife within the Crusader states.\n\nEarly life and accession\n\nRaymond III was born in 1140, the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father died in 1152, and Raymond III succeeded him as count of Tripoli. At the time of his accession, Raymond III was still a minor, and his mother, Hodierna, served as regent until he came of age.\n\nConflicts with Muslim states\n\nRaymond III faced numerous conflicts with Muslim states during his reign. In 1157, he allied with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to launch an attack on Shaizar, a city controlled by the Zengid dynasty. The campaign was unsuccessful, and Raymond III was forced to retreat.\n\nIn 1164, Raymond III joined the forces of the Kingdom of Jerusalem in a campaign against the city of Banias, which was controlled by Nur al-Din, the Zengid ruler of Aleppo. The Crusader forces were defeated, and Raymond III was captured and imprisoned. He was released in 1173, after paying a ransom.\n\nInternal strife within the Crusader states\n\nRaymond III also faced internal strife within the Crusader states. In 1157, he supported the claims of Baldwin III of Jerusalem to the throne of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, against the claims of his mother, Melisende. This led to a civil war within the Kingdom of Jerusalem, which was eventually resolved in favor of Baldwin III.\n\nIn 1174, Raymond III supported the claims of Bohemond III of Antioch to the throne of the Principality of Antioch, against the claims of his regent, Raymond of Antioch. This led to a conflict within the Principality of Antioch, which was eventually resolved in favor of Bohemond III.\n\nLater years and death\n\nRaymond III spent his later years defending the County of Tripoli against Muslim attacks. In 1187, he was killed in the Battle of Hattin, fought between the Crusader forces and the Ayyubid Sultanate. The Battle of Hattin was a decisive victory for the Muslim forces, and it marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Holy Land.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\nConflicts with Muslim states\n\nRaymond III faced numerous conflicts with Muslim states during his reign. In 1157, he allied with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to launch an attack on Shaizar, a city controlled by the Zengid dynasty. The campaign was unsuccessful, and Raymond III was forced to retreat.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\nInternal strife within the Crusader states\n\nRaymond III also faced internal strife within the Crusader states. In 1157, he supported the claims of Baldwin III of Jerusalem to the throne of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, against the claims of his mother, Melisende. This led to a civil war within the Kingdom of Jerusalem, which was eventually resolved in favor of Baldwin III.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\nLater years and death\n\nRaymond III spent his later years defending the County of Tripoli against Muslim attacks. In 1187, he was killed in the Battle of Hattin, fought between the Crusader forces and the Ayyubid Sultanate. The Battle of Hattin was a decisive victory for the Muslim forces, and it marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -9.107174992095679e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001829695247579366}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.5162993669509888}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -6.198863957251888e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "lived", "logprob": -4.818099021911621}, {"toke